5:[["$","$L10","5.5",{"topic":{"id":"5.5","title":"Cyber Terrorism and National Security","minutes":12,"summary":"Distinguish terror-related cyber conduct from ordinary cybercrime, hacktivism, espionage, and lawful dissent.","sections":[{"title":"The legal definition matters","body":["IT Act section 66F addresses specified acts with intent to threaten India’s unity, integrity, security, or sovereignty, or to strike terror, using the means and conditions stated in the provision. Unauthorized access or damage alone does not establish the full offence. BNS section 113 contains a separate terrorism framework with its own ingredients and procedural implications.","A politically motivated website defacement is not automatically cyber terrorism. Examine the intended and actual effects, target, means, and statutory wording. Conversely, a serious attack on critical infrastructure may require analysis beyond ordinary fraud or mischief. Avoid sensational labels that prejudge the evidence or dilute the legal definition."]},{"title":"Critical infrastructure and cascading effects","body":["Critical sectors can depend on interconnected digital and physical systems. A disruption may cascade through power, communications, transport, finance, and health services. Identify dependencies, safety implications, and recovery priorities. Section 70 of the IT Act concerns protected-system declarations; not every important website is automatically a protected system under that provision.","National security preparedness includes sectoral coordination, exercised response plans, backups, supply-chain risk management, and clear public communication. Technical controls should be paired with human procedures and legal authority. Overcollection of unrelated personal data is not justified merely by invoking national security."]},{"title":"Investigation with safeguards","body":["Preserve evidence of intent, coordination, target selection, and effects where relevant. A suspect’s speech or association does not by itself prove a terrorist act. Apply the statutory elements and constitutional safeguards, and distinguish intelligence assessments from admissible proof. Special procedures require careful legal review.","Coordinate victim protection, service continuity, and evidence preservation. Public statements should avoid unsupported attribution and unnecessary disclosure of sensitive infrastructure details. After the incident, review dependencies and controls rather than assuming that a single arrest or takedown eliminates systemic risk."]}],"practice":{"title":"Apply the threshold carefully","scenario":"A fictional group defaces a public information page and separately threatens a safety-critical control system.","steps":["Separate the two sets of conduct and evidence.","Identify the statutory ingredients for each candidate offence.","Assess safety and critical-infrastructure dependencies.","State what evidence of intent and effects is still missing."],"debrief":"The same investigation can contain different conduct with different legal thresholds. A single “terror” label obscures that distinction."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bns"],"sources":["itact","bns","constitution","nist-csf"],"labId":"$undefined","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain the legal definition matters and apply it to an investigation.","Explain critical infrastructure and cascading effects and apply it to an investigation.","Explain investigation with safeguards and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.5","prompt":"What is necessary before calling an incident cyber terrorism?","options":["A political slogan only.","Analysis of the applicable statutory elements and supporting evidence.","Any website defacement.","Use of an encrypted connection."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The label depends on defined legal ingredients, not the attacker’s chosen label or the mere use of a computer.","unit":5}}}]]
